# Earth's Motions: Rotation, Revolution, and the Making of Seasons

> The Earth never sits still: it spins daily on a tilted axis and
> races yearly around the Sun. Together these two motions — plus
> that crucial 23.5° tilt — produce day and night, the seasons, the
> march of the noon shadow, and the two special latitudes where the
> Sun stands overhead.

## 1. Rotation: The Daily Spin

[definition]
**Rotation** is Earth's spin on its axis — west to east
(counterclockwise viewed from above the North Pole) — taking
**23 hours 56 minutes 4 seconds** (one sidereal day) per rotation.
The 24-hour solar day is slightly longer because Earth also moves
along its orbit while spinning.
[/definition]

### Consequences of rotation

- **Day and night:** the illuminated hemisphere faces the Sun; the
  terminator (circle of illumination) sweeps west as Earth turns
  east.
- **The Sun's apparent journey:** sunrise in the east, noon high,
  sunset in the west.
- **Time zones:** 360° ÷ 24 h = 15° per hour. IST (UTC+5:30) is
  referenced to 82.5° E (near Mirzapur, UP).
- **Coriolis effect:** moving bodies deflect right in the Northern
  Hemisphere, left in the Southern — steering winds and ocean
  currents (see the Pressure and Winds lesson).
- **The bulge:** rotation makes Earth an **oblate spheroid** —
  equatorial diameter ~43 km larger than polar.

## 2. Revolution: The Yearly Journey

Earth orbits the Sun at ~107,000 km/h along a slightly **elliptical**
path (eccentricity 0.017 — nearly circular):

- **Perihelion** (closest, ~147 million km): around **3 January**
- **Aphelion** (farthest, ~152 million km): around **4 July**

[important]
Seasons are NOT caused by distance from the Sun — Earth is closest
to the Sun in January, the Northern Hemisphere's winter! The cause
is the **axial tilt**: 23.5° from the perpendicular to the orbital
plane, pointing steadily at Polaris all year.
[/important]

## 3. The Tilt Makes the Seasons

With a 23.5° tilt, as Earth revolves:

- **June solstice (~21 June):** the North Pole tilts sunward. The
  Sun's rays strike vertically at the **Tropic of Cancer (23.5° N)**
  — longest day, summer in the Northern Hemisphere.
- **December solstice (~22 December):** the tilt reverses; vertical
  Sun over the **Tropic of Capricorn (23.5° S)** — Northern winter.
- **Equinoxes (~20 March, ~22–23 September):** no tilt component
  along the Sun–Earth line; the Sun is overhead at the **Equator**
  and day equals night everywhere ('equi-nox').

| Event | Date | Vertical Sun at | NH season |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Spring (vernal) equinox | ~20 March | Equator | Spring begins |
| Summer solstice | ~21 June | Tropic of Cancer | Summer begins |
| Autumnal equinox | ~23 September | Equator | Autumn begins |
| Winter solstice | ~22 December | Tropic of Capricorn | Winter begins |

### Why tilt means warmth

Two effects compound in summer: (1) **higher solar angle** — the
same energy concentrates on less ground (shine a torch straight vs
slanted); (2) **longer days** — more hours of sunlight. Winter
reverses both.

@chart bar title="Approximate daylight hours on key dates at 40° N"
labels:
21 Mar
21 Jun
23 Sep
22 Dec

data:
12
15
12
9
@/chart

[tip]
At the equator days stay ~12 h year-round; at the poles the Sun
stays up six months and sets for six. Between the tropics the Sun is
overhead twice a year; it is NEVER overhead poleward of the tropics
— the tropics mark exactly how far the vertical ray travels.
[/tip]

## 4. Latitudes Drawn by the Motions

| Line | Latitude | Reason it exists |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Equator | 0° | Great circle equidistant from the poles |
| Tropic of Cancer | 23.5° N | Northernmost vertical Sun (June) |
| Tropic of Capricorn | 23.5° S | Southernmost vertical Sun (December) |
| Arctic Circle | 66.5° N | Midnight sun on 21 June |
| Antarctic Circle | 66.5° S | Midnight sun on 22 December |

## 5. Eclipses: Shadows in the Sky

- **Lunar eclipse:** Earth blocks sunlight, its shadow falls on the
  full Moon — the Moon turns coppery ("blood moon"). Safe to watch.
- **Solar eclipse:** the new Moon blocks the Sun; viewers inside the
  umbral shadow see totality. Never view without certified filters.

Eclipses are rare because the Moon's orbit tilts ~5° to the
ecliptic — shadows usually miss. The two points where the orbital
planes cross are the **nodes**, and eclipses occur only when the new
or full Moon is near a node (eclipse seasons, ~every 6 months).

## 6. The Slow Motions: Why Geography Is Never Finished

- **Precession:** Earth's axis wobbles like a spinning top,
  tracing a circle every ~25,800 years — Polaris has not always been
  the pole star.
- **Axial wobble (nutation)** and **Milankovitch cycles** (tilt
  22.1°–24.5° every ~41,000 years; orbital eccentricity ~100,000
  years) pace the ice ages.
- **Tidal braking:** days lengthen ~1.7 ms/century as the Moon
  recedes — in the Devonian, days were ~22 hours long.

[summary]
Earth rotates west-to-east daily (making day–night, time zones, the
Coriolis deflection, and its equatorial bulge) and revolves
elliptically yearly (perihelion in January, aphelion in July).
Seasons come from the steady 23.5° tilt: solstices at the tropics,
equinoxes over the equator, with high sun angle plus long days
making summer. Latitudes from the Arctic Circle to the tropics are
direct products of the tilt; eclipses need Moon near its nodes; and
slow precession and Milankovitch rhythms set the long drumbeat of
climate.
[/summary]
